   "id": "c007",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Found:** nothing missing, no duplicates, every step is 300 seconds (5 minutes), every VM has all 134 readings.\n",
    "**Decision:** no cleaning or filling needed."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c008",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3. Do VMs come and go?\n",
    "**Question:** Does the number of active VMs change?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 14,
   "id": "c009",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 700x300 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    },
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "VMs created after the start   : 0\n",
      "VMs deleted before last reading: 0\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "vm_count = tr.groupby(\"timestamp\")[\"vm_id\"].nunique(),
    "\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(7, 3))\n",
    "sns.lineplot(x=hours, y=vm_count.values)\n",
    "plt.ylim(0, vm_count.max() * 1.2)\n",
    "plt.title(\"Number of active VMs\")\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"hours\"); plt.ylabel(\"VMs\")\n",
    "plt.savefig(\"../reports/figures/03_vm_count.png\", dpi=150, bbox_inches=\"tight\")\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"VMs created after the start   :\", (meta[\"timestamp_vm_created\"] > 0).sum())\n",
    "print(\"VMs deleted before last reading:\", (meta[\"timestamp_vm_deleted\"] <= cpu[\"timestamp\"].max()).sum())"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c010",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Found:** a flat line, exactly 10,000 VMs at all times. No VM is created or deleted.\n",
    "**Why:** the dataset only keeps VMs that lived through the whole window.\n",
    "**Decision:** forecast the count with persistence (error = 0) and spend the effort on CPU.\n",
    "**Limitation:** real clouds have VMs starting and stopping, and this dataset hides that."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c011",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4. What does average CPU look like over time?\n",
    "**Question:** Is it noisy, trending or smooth?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 15,
   "id": "c012",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1300x300 with 3 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    },
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "average level      : 9.41\n",
      "lowest / highest   : 9.11 / 9.91\n",
      "typical 5-min jump : 0.158\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "by_time = tr.groupby(\"timestamp\")[\"avg_cpu\"]\n",
    "mean_cpu = by_time.mean()\n",
    "median_cpu = by_time.median()\n",
    "p95_cpu = by_time.quantile(0.95)\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 3, figsize=(13, 3))\n",
    "sns.lineplot(x=hours, y=mean_cpu.values, ax=axes[0]);   axes[0].set_title(\"Mean\")\n",
    "sns.lineplot(x=hours, y=median_cpu.values, ax=axes[1]); axes[1].set_title(\"Median\")\n",
    "sns.lineplot(x=hours, y=p95_cpu.values, ax=axes[2]);    axes[2].set_title(\"95th percentile\")\n",
    "for ax in axes:\n",
    "    ax.set_xlabel(\"hours\")\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.savefig(\"../reports/figures/04_aggregate.png\", dpi=150, bbox_inches=\"tight\")\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "step = mean_cpu.diff().dropna()",
    "print(\"average level      :\", round(mean_cpu.mean(), 2))\n",
    "print(\"lowest / highest   :\", round(mean_cpu.min(), 2), \"/\", round(mean_cpu.max(), 2))\n",
    "print(\"typical 5-min jump :\", round(step.std(), 3))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c013",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Found:** the mean stays near 9.4% and moves only about ±0.2 points.\n",
    "**Why:** averaging 10,000 VMs cancels most random ups and downs.\n",
    "**Decision:** easy to forecast, so a simple model may be enough. But a calm average can hide risk (see section 8)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c014",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 5. Is there a pattern in time?\n",
    "**Question:** Trend? Repeating cycle? Pull back to the average?\n",
    "**What I did:** autocorrelation (ACF) = how similar the series is to itself *k* steps earlier. Computed with pandas, plotted as bars."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 16,
   "id": "c015",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 800x300 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    },
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "average of first half : 9.39\n",
      "average of second half: 9.44\n",
      "ACF at lag 1, 6, 12   : [np.float64(0.67), np.float64(0.48), np.float64(0.37)]\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "lags = range(1, 31)\n",
    "acf = [mean_cpu.autocorr(k) for k in lags]",
    "\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(8, 3))\n",
    "sns.barplot(x=list(lags), y=acf, color=\"steelblue\")\n",
    "band = 1.96 / np.sqrt(len(mean_cpu))",
    "plt.axhline(band, color=\"red\", linestyle=\"--\")\n",
    "plt.axhline(-band, color=\"red\", linestyle=\"--\")\n",
    "plt.title(\"Autocorrelation of the mean CPU\")\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"lag (5-minute steps)\"); plt.ylabel(\"autocorrelation\")\n",
    "plt.savefig(\"../reports/figures/05_acf.png\", dpi=150, bbox_inches=\"tight\")\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "half = len(mean_cpu) // 2\n",
    "print(\"average of first half :\", round(mean_cpu.iloc[:half].mean(), 2))\n",
    "print(\"average of second half:\", round(mean_cpu.iloc[half:].mean(), 2))\n",
    "print(\"ACF at lag 1, 6, 12   :\", [round(mean_cpu.autocorr(k), 2) for k in (1, 6, 12)])"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c016",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Found:** ACF is about 0.68 at lag 1 and fades slowly (0.53 at lag 6, 0.44 at lag 12). The two halves have similar averages, so no clear trend.\n",
    "**Why:** the series wanders a little but keeps returning to its average (mean reversion).\n",
    "**Decision:**\n",
    "- try models that pull back to the average (AR, ARMA);\n",
    "- persistence should be weak, because the last value is noisy;\n",
    "- a daily cycle needs 24 hours and I only have about 11, so I cannot test it."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c017",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 6. What does CPU of a single VM look like?\n",
    "**Question:** Is load spread across many VMs, or caused by a few very busy ones?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 17,
   "id": "c018",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1100x300 with 2 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    },
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "mean   : 9.41\n",
      "median : 5.12\n",
      "share of all CPU used by the busiest 1% of VMs: 9.6 %\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "vm_average = tr.groupby(\"vm_id\")[\"avg_cpu\"].mean()",
    "\n",
    "fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(11, 3))\n",
    "sns.histplot(tr[\"avg_cpu\"], bins=100, ax=axes[0]);  axes[0].set_title(\"All readings\")\n",
    "sns.histplot(vm_average, bins=100, ax=axes[1]);     axes[1].set_title(\"Average per VM\")\n",
    "for ax in axes:\n",
    "    ax.set_yscale(\"log\")",
    "    ax.set_xlabel(\"avg_cpu %\")\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.savefig(\"../reports/figures/06_distribution.png\", dpi=150, bbox_inches=\"tight\")\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "busiest = vm_average.sort_values(ascending=False).head(len(vm_average) // 100)",
    "print(\"mean   :\", round(tr[\"avg_cpu\"].mean(), 2))\n",
    "print(\"median :\", round(tr[\"avg_cpu\"].median(), 2))\n",
    "print(\"share of all CPU used by the busiest 1% of VMs:\", round(busiest.sum() / vm_average.sum() * 100, 1), \"%\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c019",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Found:** most VMs are quiet (median about 5%), a few are busy (mean about 9.4%). The busiest 1% of VMs use only about 10% of all CPU.\n",
    "**Why:** load is spread broadly. My first guess, \"a few hot VMs drive the mean\", was wrong, and I corrected it with the data.\n",
    "**Decision:** a fleet-level forecast makes sense."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c020",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 7. What happens in busy and quiet periods?\n",
    "**Question:** When the average is high, do many VMs rise a little, or a few rise a lot?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 18,
   "id": "c021",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1100x300 with 2 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    },
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "gap between peak and low average: 0.69\n",
      "share of VMs that are higher in peaks: 57.8 %\n",
      "share of the total change from the 100 biggest movers: 30.5 %\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "peak_times = mean_cpu[mean_cpu >= mean_cpu.quantile(0.9)].index",
    "low_times = mean_cpu[mean_cpu <= mean_cpu.quantile(0.1)].index",
    "\n",
    "peak_by_vm = tr[tr[\"timestamp\"].isin(peak_times)].groupby(\"vm_id\")[\"avg_cpu\"].mean()\n",
    "low_by_vm = tr[tr[\"timestamp\"].isin(low_times)].groupby(\"vm_id\")[\"avg_cpu\"].mean()\n",
    "difference = peak_by_vm - low_by_vm",
    "\n",
    "fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(11, 3))\n",
    "sns.lineplot(x=hours, y=mean_cpu.values, ax=axes[0])\n",
    "sns.scatterplot(x=(peak_times - train_times[0]) / 3600, y=mean_cpu[peak_times].values, color=\"red\", label=\"top 10%\", ax=axes[0])\n",
    "sns.scatterplot(x=(low_times - train_times[0]) / 3600, y=mean_cpu[low_times].values, color=\"green\", label=\"bottom 10%\", ax=axes[0])\n",
    "axes[0].set_title(\"Busy and quiet periods\"); axes[0].set_xlabel(\"hours\")\n",
    "sns.histplot(difference.clip(-5, 5), bins=80, ax=axes[1])\n",
    "axes[1].axvline(0, color=\"black\")\n",
    "axes[1].set_title(\"Change per VM (peak minus low)\"); axes[1].set_xlabel(\"points (cut at ±5)\")\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.savefig(\"../reports/figures/07_peaks.png\", dpi=150, bbox_inches=\"tight\")\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"gap between peak and low average:\", round(mean_cpu[peak_times].mean() - mean_cpu[low_times].mean(), 2))\n",
    "print(\"share of VMs that are higher in peaks:\", round((difference > 0).mean() * 100, 1), \"%\")\n",
    "biggest = difference.abs().sort_values(ascending=False).head(100).index",
    "print(\"share of the total change from the 100 biggest movers:\", round(difference[biggest].sum() / difference.sum() * 100, 1), \"%\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c022",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Found:** Peaks are mostly a broad, modest rise. About 58% of VMs run higher in the peak than in the quiet periods, and the typical change per VM is small. But the 100 biggest movers (1% of VMs) account for about 30% of the net change, so a small group of heavy VMs adds a lot on top. The peak is one sustained episode (about hour 4.5 to 5.7) and not scattered spikes. The gap between peak and low average is 0.69 points, about 7% of the average level.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why:** Since the rise is sustained, the level takes time to come back down, which matches the autocorrelation in section 5.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Decision:** A fleet-wide percentage safety margin (a few percent above the forecast) covers the broad rise. I also watch the small group of heavy movers separately, because they cause a large share of the swing."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c023",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 8. Where is the real risk?\n",
    "**Question:** The average is calm. Are there VMs that run hot?\n",
    "**What I did:** counted VMs above 50% and 80%, and compared `max_cpu` (highest point inside 5 minutes) with `avg_cpu`."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 19,
   "id": "c024",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1300x300 with 3 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    },
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "average % of VMs above 50%: 3.06\n",
      "average % of VMs above 80%: 1.27\n",
      "VMs above 80% at least once: 437\n",
      "VMs above 80% in more than half of the intervals: 113\n",
      "average max_cpu: 18.44 | average avg_cpu: 9.41\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "tr[\"above_50\"] = tr[\"avg_cpu\"] > 50\n",
    "tr[\"above_80\"] = tr[\"avg_cpu\"] > 80\n",
    "share = tr.groupby(\"timestamp\")[[\"above_50\", \"above_80\"]].mean() * 100",
    "hot_counts = tr[tr[\"above_80\"]].groupby(\"vm_id\").size()",
    "\n",
    "fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 3, figsize=(13, 3))\n",
    "sns.lineplot(x=hours, y=share[\"above_50\"].values, label=\"above 50%\", ax=axes[0])\n",
    "sns.lineplot(x=hours, y=share[\"above_80\"].values, label=\"above 80%\", ax=axes[0])\n",
    "axes[0].set_title(\"% of VMs that are hot\"); axes[0].set_xlabel(\"hours\")\n",
    "sns.histplot(tr[\"max_cpu\"] - tr[\"avg_cpu\"], bins=100, ax=axes[1])\n",
    "axes[1].set_yscale(\"log\"); axes[1].set_title(\"max_cpu minus avg_cpu\"); axes[1].set_xlabel(\"points\")\n",
    "sns.histplot(hot_counts, bins=30, ax=axes[2])\n",
    "axes[2].set_yscale(\"log\"); axes[2].set_title(\"Intervals above 80% per hot VM\"); axes[2].set_xlabel(\"intervals\")\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.savefig(\"../reports/figures/08_risk.png\", dpi=150, bbox_inches=\"tight\")\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"average % of VMs above 50%:\", round(share[\"above_50\"].mean(), 2))\n",
    "print(\"average % of VMs above 80%:\", round(share[\"above_80\"].mean(), 2))\n",
    "print(\"VMs above 80% at least once:\", len(hot_counts))\n",
    "print(\"VMs above 80% in more than half of the intervals:\", (hot_counts > len(train_times) / 2).sum())\n",
    "print(\"average max_cpu:\", round(tr[\"max_cpu\"].mean(), 2), \"| average avg_cpu:\", round(tr[\"avg_cpu\"].mean(), 2))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c025",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Found:** About 3.1% of VMs are above 50% CPU and 1.3% are above 80% at any time. The hot set is mostly persistent: 437 VMs crossed 80% at least once, but 113 of them stayed above 80% in more than half of the intervals, while about 150 only touched it briefly. The share above 50% rises to about 3.8% during the peak seen in section 7, and the share above 80% stays flat. Mean `max_cpu` is 18.4% against 9.4% for `avg_cpu`, about double, with gaps above 90 points in the tail.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why:** `avg_cpu` hides short spikes inside each 5-minute interval, so sizing on averages understates short-term demand. Roughly 1% of VMs are always hot, and a smaller group spikes briefly.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Decision:** The capacity rule needs two parts. (1) A fleet-level safety margin on the forecast. Because `max_cpu` is about twice `avg_cpu`, the margin should be larger than the forecast error alone would suggest. (2) A per-VM rule for the roughly 110 persistently hot VMs, such as monitoring them, resizing them, or giving them dedicated capacity. Tail measures (share above 80%, 95th percentile) should be tracked next to the mean."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c026",
   "metadata": {},
