   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 10. What could leak future information?\n",
    "\n",
    "**Not used in models, because they leak the future:**\n",
    "\n",
    "- `avg_cpu`, `max_cpu` and `p95_max_cpu` in the metadata file are calculated over the whole VM lifetime, which includes the holdout.\n",
    "- `timestamp_vm_deleted` reveals the future (when a VM ends). I only used it to describe the data.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Not used, because they carry no information:**\n",
    "\n",
    "- `timestamp_vm_created` is always 0.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Safe to use:**\n",
    "\n",
    "- `vm_category`, cores and memory are fixed labels known in advance.\n",
    "- Past CPU values from the training window are known at forecast time.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Decision:** models only see past values of the target series."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c030",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 11. Summary\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Data is clean:** no gaps, duplicates or missing values, so no cleaning is needed.\n",
    "- **VM count is constant:** 10,000 at every time, so I use persistence for the count.\n",
    "- **Average CPU is smooth:** tiny 5-minute changes, so simple models can work.\n",
    "- **It returns to its average:** ACF 0.68 / 0.53 / 0.44 and no trend, so I try AR/ARMA, ETS and ridge.\n",
    "- **Only 9 hours of data:** no daily cycle is visible, so a seasonal model is only an experiment.\n",
    "- **Load is spread widely:** the busiest 1% of VMs use about 10% of CPU, so a fleet-level forecast is fine.\n",
    "- **The average hides spikes:** `max_cpu` is far above `avg_cpu`, so I use a safety margin and tail measures.\n",
    "- **Some metadata leaks the future:** it covers the holdout period, so it is excluded.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Next:** compare models with time-aware validation, then build the capacity-planning rule."
   ]
  }
